    "case_id": "293",
    "case_summary": "A 50-year-old man presented in 2011 for evaluation of erythrocytosis, which had been presumed to be due to polycythemia vera for the past 13 years. A few months after presentation, the patient developed dyspnea. In 2012, the patient developed scattered telangiectasias on the chest. Over the next 4 years, the patient remained clinically stable until his intrapulmonary shunting increased significantly.\n\n- Oxygen saturation of 90% to 92% with clubbing- Scattered telangiectasias on the chest- Telangiectasias with classic spider angioma morphology on chest and back- Purple angiomatous papules on the lips resembling venous lakes\n\n- Laboratory findings:  * IgG κ monoclonal protein levels: 910 mg/dL  * Erythropoietin levels: elevated (specific value not provided)  - Imaging/Other tests:  * Intrapulmonary shunting measurements:    - Initial: 12.3%    - Later increased to: 40.5%    - Final measurement: 55.6%  * Perinephric fluid collections present  - Images described:  Title: Telangiectasias on the Lips, Chest, and Back  Description: A) Lips with several purple angiomatous papules resembling venous lakes               B,C) Scattered telangiectasias with classic spider angioma morphology on chest and back               D) Close-up view of telangiectasias on the back",
